%                                                                              %
%==============================================================================%
%                                                                              %
%                       INTRODUCTION TO THE PDF2.0 FORMAT                      %
%                                                                              %
%------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
%                                                                              %
%   1 ............................................................. OVERVIEW   %
%   1.1 ........................................................... OBJECTS    %
%   1.1.1 ........................................................ BOOLEAN     %
%   1.1.2 ........................................................ NUMERIC     %
%   1.1.3 ......................................................... STRING     %
%   1.1.4 .......................................................... NAMES     %
%   1.1.5 ......................................................... ARRAYS     %
%   1.1.6 ................................................... DICTIONARIES     %
%   1.1.7 ............................................ INDIRECT REFERENCES     %
%   1.2 ........................................................... STREAMS    %
%                                                                              %
%   2 ....................................................... FILE STRUCTURE   %
%   2.1 ............................................................ HEADER    %
%   2.2 .............................................................. BODY    %
%   2.3 ............................................. CROSS-REFERENCE TABLE    %
%   2.4 ........................................................... TRAILER    %
%                                                                              %
%   3 .............................................................. OUTLINE   %
%   3.1 ................................................. GRAPHIC OPERATORS    %
%                                                                              %
%   4 ............................................................ RESOURCES   %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%   ................................ SOURCES ...............................   %
%                                                                              %
%------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
%                                                                              %
%     Congratulations, you found the hidden vault. This paper was created, as  %
% you can now clearly see, entirely from scratch (not by hand mind you),       %
% and so, what better than to detail how to do so, within a PDF.               %
%     The goal of this little informational, is to get the necessary           %
% information one may need to start reading other PDFs source code, and be     %
% start learning by reading.                                                   %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%         OVERVIEW                                                             %
%                                                                              %
%     The core of the PDF format can be broken up into two types of things,    %
% &OBJECTS and &STREAMS.                                                       %
%                                                                              %
%             OBJECTS                                                          %
%      &OBJECTS are the base of everything. The are data structures, that hold %
% what ever data type you need. This is used to define the structure of your   %
% document, including: size, pages, fonts to use, where everything is, etc.    %
%                                                                              %
%   ` 1 0 obj<<...data...>>                                                    %
%   ` endobj                                                                   %
%                                                                              %
%     The first number is a positive whole number, that allows the object to   %
% be referenced. The second number is the generation number, for when the      %
% the object is updated.                                                       %
%     Objects have multiple data types. Objects can hold only one type (with   %
% exception to streams), but these types can hold more data types.             %
%                                                                              %
%                 BOOLEAN                                                      %
%     These shows as the keywords: `true` or `false`.                          %
%                                                                              %
%                 NUMERIC                                                      %
%     These come in two forms: integers and real numbers. Integers are numbers %
% that can be positive or negative, but must not carry any decimals. While     %
% real numbers can have anynumber of numbers on either side of a period.       %
%                                                                              %
%   ` Integers: 123    43445  +17    -98     0                                 %
%   `     Real: 34.5   -3.62  +123.6 4.     -.002  0                           %
%                                                                              %
%                 STRING                                                       %
%     These can be represented in to maners: literal strings, and hexadecimal. %
% The first uses parenthesis, `(` and `)`. Refer to [0] for more.              %
%                                                                              %
%   ` (Hello, World)                                                           %
%   ` <48656C6C6F2C20576F726C64>                                               %
%                                                                              %
%                 NAMES                                                        %
%     These are mainly used in dictionaries, and are identified with solidus,  %
% or a forward slash. There are specifications to what can be specified as a   %
% Name, it boils down to letters, and some special charactors. Refer to [0].   %
%                                                                              %
%                 ARRAYS                                                       %
%     These allow for multiple data types, does not need to be the same type,  %
% To be stored. It is denoted by the Square Brackets, `[` and `]`.             %
%                                                                              %
%   ` [549 3.14 false (Ralph) /SomeName]                                       %
%                                                                              %
%                 DICTIONARIES                                                 %
%     Much like &ARRAYS they store multiple data types, but this time,         %
% they will be stored in pairs, where the first entry must be a &NAME. They    %
% are written inside a pair of double angle brackets, `<<` and `>>`.           %
%                                                                              %
%   ` <</Type /Example                                                         %
%   `     /Subtype /DictionaryExample                                          %
%   `     /Version 0.01                                                        %
%   `     /IntegerItem 12                                                      %
%   `     /StringItem (a string)                                               %
%   `     /Subdictionary <<                                                    %
%   `         /Item1 0.4                                                       %
%   `         /Item2 true                                                      %
%   `         /LastItem (not !)                                                %
%   `         /VeryLastItem (OK)                                               %
%   `     >>                                                                   %
%   ` >>                                                                       %
%                                                                              %
%                 NULL                                                         %
%     This type is simply denoted by the keyword `null`.                       %
%                                                                              %
%                 INDIRECT REFERENCES                                          %
%     This type is a object identifier that allow objects to be refered to     %
% from another location. This shares the numbers that go before an object.     %
% These are denoted with the letter `R`, instead of the objects `obj`.         %
%                                                                              %
%   ` 12 0 R                                                                   %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%             STREAMS                                                          %
%     Streams hold data, this can range from anything from, text or fonts, to  %
% images and videos. The &OBJECTS are used to define what to do with the       %
% Streams. This data can also be compressed or other wise encoded.             %
%                                                                              %
%   ` 1 0 obj<</Length 49>>                                                    %
%   ` stream                                                                   %
%   ` /F1 10 Tf BT 100 100 Td 0 Tr (Hello, World!)Tj ET                        %
%   ` endstream                                                                %
%   ` endobj                                                                   %
%                                                                              %
%   NOTE: this guide does not cover encoding streams.                          %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%         FILE STRUCTURE                                                       %
%                                                                              %
%     The struce of a PDF can broadly be split into four parts: The Header,    %
% Body, Cross-reference table and Trailer. As the figure  .-----------------.  %
% suggests, the body is the most important and largest    |     HEADER      |  %
% section of the file. Since it contains the meat. Many   |-----------------|  %
% PDF readers work with the last two broken. [Fig. 1]     |                 |  %
%                                                         |                 |  %
%             HEADER                                      |      BODY       |  %
%     Each PDF starts with five charactors, `%PDF-`, with |                 |  %
% the PDF version. For example this document is using     |                 |  %
% version `2.0`.                                          |-----------------|  %
%                                                         | CROSS-REFERENCE |  %
%             BODY                                        |      TABLE      |  %
%     The body is a list of &OBJECTS that represent the   |-----------------|  %
% contents of the document.                               |     TRAILER     |  %
%                                                         `-----------------'  %
%             CROSS-REFERENCE TABLE                          Fig. 1: File      %
%     The table has a list of references and indexes to    Structure Outline   %
% objects. The first column specifies the byte offset,    .                 .  %
% while the second column is for the generation.                               %
%     The first two numbers, after the start of the table `xref`, indicate     %
% the starting object number, and the count of objects.                        %
%                                                                              %
%   ` xref                                                                     %
%   ` 0 6                                                                      %
%   ` 0000000000 65535 f                                                       %
%   ` 0000000017 00000 n                                                       %
%   ` 0000000081 00000 n                                                       %
%   ` 0000000000 00007 f                                                       %
%   ` 0000000331 00000 n                                                       %
%   ` 0000000409 00000 n                                                       %
%                                                                              %
%     The letter `f` indicates the entry is not in use, other wise the         %
% letter `n` is used.                                                          %
%                                                                              %
%             TRAILER                                                          %
%    The trailer stows infomation related to the entire document,              %
% including: Total entries in xref table, reference to the root &OBJECT,       %
% reference to the Info dictionary, ID (basically checksum), etc.              %
%                                                                              %
%  ` trailer<</Size 22/Root 2 0 R/Info 1 0 R>>                                 %
%  ` startxref                                                                 %
%  ` 18799                                                                     %
%  ` %%EOF                                                                     %
%                                                                              %
%    At the end of the trailer is for the byte offset of the last xref         %
% tables, followed by the End Of File (EOF) Marker.                            %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%         OUTLINE                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%     Since we now have the basics understood, we can start putting objects    %
% down. First we create the Root Object [Obj. 1], which points to the Pages    %
% Object [Obj. 2].                                .                          . %
%     From the Pages Object, we are directed to            [Obj. 1]            %
% each page we specify. Each Page Object stores                |               %
% then stores the infomation related to that           .-> [Obj. 2]            %
% page, including: Contents, Parent, Resources,        \    /    \             %
% Annotations, etc. [Obj. 5,6]                         [Obj. 5] [Obj. 6]->...  %
%     From the Pages Object, the contents are           /    \                 %
% Referenced to an object that holds a stream.     [Obj. 8] [Obj. 4]           %
% This stream may be encoded, but for ease of     .                          . %
% reading this document does not encode anything.      Fig. 2: Simplified      %
% The stream uses graphic operators to dictate     Directed Graph of Document  %
% what to draw. [Obj. 7,8]                        .                          . %
%    The Pages Object also includes a key                                      %
% that defines resources. These resources are external objects, like fonts and %
% other media, that may be shared between pages. [Obj. 4]                      %
%                                                                              %
%             GRAPHIC OPERATORS                                                %
%     These operators operate differently from normal programing languages.    %
% The data types are first expressed, then the operators terminate.            %
%     There are multiple types of Graphic Operators. For now, we will focus    %
% on the Text Operators, which will allow us to get a simple outline.          %
% The following are the basic operators needed to create and position text.    %
%                                                                              %
%   ` BT                                                                       %
%   `     /F13 12 Tf                                                           %
%   `     288 720 Td                                                           %
%   `     ( ABC ) Tj                                                           %
%   ` ET                                                                       %
%                                                                              %
%   * The `BT` and `ET` specify the Start and End of the text mode.            %
%   * The `Tf` specifies the Texts font, and the size of that font.            %
%   * The `Td` specifies the Texts destination, with width and height.         %
%   * The `Tj` specifies the Text to jolt down.                                %
%                                                                              %
%     All of these operators apply from the bottom left of the page upwards,   %
% Instead of the more common top left. This means that higher numbers make     %
% there way to the top right.                                                  %
%     The PDF Association, has a couple of cheat sheets for these operators    %
% and others, on their website. [4]                                            %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%                                                                              %
%         REFERENCES                                                           %
%                                                                              %
%     There are not a whole lot of resources for this kind of work. There are  %
% three sources that I used to be able to create this document:                %
%                                                                              %
%   * The ISO standards guide: ISO 3200-1:2020 [0]                             %
%   * Other peoples pdf files: LaTeX [2], Typst [3], and PDF-Association [1]   %
%   * The PDF-Association: Cheat sheets [4]                                    %
%                                                                              %
%     This means, you can really take two paths, read boring standards,        %
% or read generated PDFs. Really both are necessary, as there are a lot of     %
% discrepencies between the standard, and the implementations.                 %
%                                                                              %
%==============================================================================%
%                                                                              %
%                                   SOURCES                                    %
%                                                                              %
%------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
%                                                                              %
% [0] ISO, "Portable document format", ISO 3200-2:2020, 2020. [Standard]       %
% [1] PDF-Association, "PDF 2.0 Examples," github.com. [Online]                %
%     Available: https://github.com/pdf-association/pdf20examples              %
%     Accessed: Apr. 12, 2026                                                  %
% [2] "LaTeX", latex-project.org. [Online]                                     %
%     Available: https://www.latex-project.org                                 %
% [3] "Typst", typst.app. [Online]                                             %
%     Available: https://typst.app                                             %
% [4] PDF-Association, "PDF Cheat Sheets v2.0," pdfa.org. [Online]             %
%     Available: https://pdfa.org/resource/pdf-cheat-sheets                    %
%                                                                              %
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

% Root Object
1 0 obj<</Type/Catalog/Pages 2 0 R>>
endobj
% Pages Object
2 0 obj<</Type/Pages/Count 2/Kids[5 0 R 6 0 R]>>
endobj

% Font Object
3 0 obj<</Type/Font/Subtype/Type1/BaseFont/Courier/Encoding/WinAnsiEncoding>>
endobj
% Resources Object
4 0 obj<</Font<</F1 3 0 R>>>>
endobj

% (page #1) Title Page Object
5 0 obj<</Type/Page/Contents[7 0 R]/MediaBox[0 0 595.44 842.4]/Parent[2 0 R]/Resources 4 0 R/Annots[12 0 R 14 0 R]>>
endobj
% (page #2) Body Page Object
6 0 obj<</Type/Page/Contents[8 0 R]/MediaBox[0 0 595.44 842.4]/Parent[2 0 R]/Resources 4 0 R/Annots[9 0 R 10 0 R 11 0 R 15 0 R 16 0 R 17 0 R 18 0 R 19 0 R 20 0 R 21 0 R]>>
endobj


